1️⃣ RoleEntity → DTOs needed
✅ Required DTOs (3)
✔ RoleRequestDto
Used for:
Create role
Update role
Fields:
roleName
description

❌ No id, isDeleted, timestamps

✔ RoleResponseDto

Used for:

Get role by id

Role details page

Fields:

id

roleName

description

createdAt

updatedAt

✔ RoleListDto

Used for:

GET /roles (table, dropdown)

Fields:

id

roleName

description

🔹 No RoleUser DTO

You never expose userRoles directly

Avoid recursion & over-fetching

2️⃣ UserEntity → DTOs needed

User is more complex, so it needs more DTOs.

✅ Core DTOs (mandatory)
✔ UserRegisterDto (or UserCreateDto)

Used for:

User signup

Admin create user

Fields:

username

email

password

fullName

✔ UserResponseDto

Used for:

Get user profile

Admin view user

Fields:

id

username

email

fullName

headline

bio
profileImageUrl

contactNumber

location

accountStatus

emailVerified

createdAt

updatedAt

roles (⚠️ simplified)

👉 roles should be:

List<String> roles;


NOT RoleEntity

✔ UserListDto

Used for:

Admin user list

Search users

Fields:

id

username

email

accountStatus

✅ Functional DTOs (very important)
✔ UserLoginDto

Fields:

usernameOrEmail

password

✔ UserUpdateProfileDto

Fields:

fullName

headline

bio

profileImageUrl

contactNumber

location

❌ Never expose:

passwordHash

failedLoginAttempts

lockUntil

3️⃣ UserRoleEntity → DTOs needed

This is a join table, so DTOs are action-based, not CRUD-based.

✅ Required DTOs (2)
✔ AssignRoleDto

Used for:

Assign role to user

Fields:

userId

roleId

✔ UserRoleResponseDto

Used for:

View user roles

Admin audit

Fields:

userId

roleName

assignedAt

❌ No UserRoleRequestDto / UserRoleEntityDto
Because:

Client never creates join table directly
Service controls assignment logic